# See https://help.github.com/articles/about-codeowners/

# Global owners of the repository. Both teams are requested on any path that has
# no more-specific rule below, so shared/unclaimed code surfaces to
# performance-pacing and performance-ml together. CODEOWNERS is last-match-wins,
# so the more-specific rules below override this default for the paths they name.
* @SteelHouse/pacing @SteelHouse/performance-ml

# Performance ML-owned DAGs. These rules override the global default so PRs
# touching these paths request review from performance-ml only. Each perml DAG
# family has code in two trees -- the DAG definition under dags/ and its
# bootstrap/spark dependencies under spark_scripts/ -- so both are listed.
# Patterns are prefix globs so current and future variants of each family are
# covered automatically (e.g. media_plan, media_plan_analytics, ...). When perml
# starts a brand-new DAG family, add a dags/<family>*/ (and spark_scripts/
# <family>*/) rule here.

# inventory_forecast
/dags/inventory_forecast*/                   @SteelHouse/performance-ml
/spark_scripts/inventory_forecast*/          @SteelHouse/performance-ml

# media_plan family (media_plan, media_plan_analytics, media_plan_regeneration, ...)
/dags/media_plan*/                           @SteelHouse/performance-ml
/spark_scripts/media_plan*/                  @SteelHouse/performance-ml

# deliverability (deliverability_classification_monitor, ...; BigQuery-only today)
/dags/deliverability*/                       @SteelHouse/performance-ml
/spark_scripts/deliverability*/              @SteelHouse/performance-ml

# autotof
/dags/autotof*/                              @SteelHouse/performance-ml
/spark_scripts/autotof*/                     @SteelHouse/performance-ml

# NOTE: Shared infra (dag_utils/, spark_scripts/utils/) has no specific rule, so
# it falls under the global default and is owned by both teams.
